  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nRAP analysis this afternoon shows today's cold front currently \ndraped across the central UP as it progresses eastward. Southerly \nflow ahead of the front this morning generated widespread 20 mph \ngusts across the eastern UP and a few as high as 30 mph. Behind the \nfront this afternoon, WNW'ly surface winds show gusts to 20 mph, \nwith temperatures in the high 60s. Frontal passage has limited \ndaytime heating somewhat in the western UP, though locations in the \neastern UP are currently in the high 70s.\n\nAs this morning's ridge exits the region, a mid-level Canadian low \nsets up in conjunction with a Four Corners high. This pattern \npersists for next couple days while the Canadian low propagates \nslowly eastward. Combined anticyclonic flow from the NE quadrant of \nthe Four Corners and cyclonic flow along the southern half of the \nCanadian low creates robust NW'ly mid-level pressure gradient flow \nacross the Upper Midwest for the next few days. Weak surface ridging \nbuilds in on Wednesday for a dry day tomorrow; on Thursday, a pair \nof shortwaves orbit through but with no expected precipitation given \na lack of substantial moisture. With surface ridging building in \nover the next few days, winds will be mostly light but with some \nperiods of elevated gusts as LLJs form, dissipate, and rotate around \nthe Canadian low. \n\nWhat weather we do experience may be initiated by shortwaves \norbiting around the slow-moving Canadian low, whose surface feature \nsits in northern Ontario this afternoon. The next shortwave with \nnotable moisture and precipitation chances appears to be Friday \nevening, when the NBM shows an anomalously strong LLJ moving \noverheard. Along with this, the NBM shows up to 50% chance of SBCAPE \ngreater than 500 J/kg along the WI border Friday afternoon, and 30 \nknots of 0-500mb shear. NBM probability of 6-hr precip exceeding \n0.1\" is around 50% for at least some point on Friday afternoon and \nevening, and the 90th percentile of 6-hr distributions top out \nat 0.6\" at this point in time. All told, severe weather is not \nexpected for this event at this time.\n\nFurther out, differences in LREF clustering in 500 mb heights begin \naround Day 6 (Aug 8-9) in the timing and strength of trough entering \ninto the PNW from the Pacific (though the strength of this \ntrough is so weak such that the question is not 'how strong?' \nbut rather 'trough or zonal?'). That said, the influence on this\nregion for days 6-9 are all very similar, with the Canadian low\nand Four Corners high still in place leading to local NW'ly \nmid- level flow.",
